5. honesty — a HOSTILE seat process CAN reach B; the OS is not the wall
  ok    all four cross-project attempts were made and reported
  note  OS boundary: 4/4 hostile cross-writes SUCCEEDED — same user, no sandbox.
  note  Isolation here is by construction and contract, not OS enforcement.
  ok    the report and B's actual state agree (4 succeeded; B mutated: yes) — the test tells the truth either way
  ok    after restoring B from the snapshot: B's project tree and seat root are byte-identical to the snapshot

6. canary — can these checks detect a machinery that DOES cross over?
  ok    canary 6a: an adapter that writes its logs into B is caught by the pristine-B check
  ok    canary 6b: a bd call scoped to B's store IS visible to the B-scoped-call check
  ok    after canary 6b (fake bd only logs; B untouched): B's project tree and seat root are byte-identical to the snapshot

THE BOUNDARY: isolation between projects is by construction and contract,
not OS enforcement. Every path the machinery writes derives from its own
project root and namespaced seat root, and path-shaped arguments are
refused — but one user, no sandbox: the OS permits a hostile seat to write
anywhere, and phase 5 proved it rather than assuming otherwise.

